          "deepDive": "**AなりBなり** offers a couple of examples from an open set: *A or B or something like that — whichever*. The speaker doesn't care which; the point is that *some* such action should be taken. Hence it is typical in suggestions, advice, orders and permission: 〜すればいい, 〜てください, 〜したらどう？, 好きに〜していい.\n\nConnection (flexible): nouns, nouns + particle (外国へなりどこへなり, 先生になり親になり), and V-る (見るなり、するなり). The final element is often a question word, making the list fully open: **〜なり何なり**, **〜へなりどこへなり**, **〜になり誰になり**. A single 〜なり can also stand alone: 電話なりしてくれればよかったのに (*you could at least have phoned or something*).\n\nContrasts:\n- **AやらBやら** (N2): listing things that actually happened, often with a sense of confusion (泣くやら笑うやら). なり〜なり is about options you could choose.\n- **AとかBとか / AたりBたり**: neutral listing; no sense of 'any of these will do'.\n- **AであれBであれ** (#38): *whether A or B, it's the same* — concession rather than suggestion.\n\nThe やってみよう points: the two items should be comparable alternatives of the same kind. 人に聞くなり辞書を引くなり (two ways of finding out) — not ✗わかるなり; メールなり電話なり (two means of contact) — not ✗連絡, which is the category itself.\n\nDon't confuse with **〜なり** (#28, *as soon as*) or **〜なりに** (#18). Note that なり〜なり isn't used for past facts: ✗昨日は映画を見るなり買い物をするなりした.",
