          "deepDive": "**AはおろかB（も／さえ／まで／すら）〜ない** = *not even B, let alone A*. おろか (愚か *foolish*) here means *it would be foolish even to mention A*. A is the more obvious/bigger thing; B is the minimal, most basic thing — and even B is lacking. The sentence is usually negative and expresses surprise, dismay or criticism (regret icon).\n\nOrder matters: A = higher degree (食事, エアコン, 恋人, 起き上がること), B = lower, more basic degree (水, 扇風機, 友人, 寝返り). The やってみよう tests this: 自己紹介はおろか挨拶も (greeting is *more* basic than self-introduction), 家賃はおろか今日の食事代にも困る.\n\nConnection: nouns, or clauses nominalised with こと (起き上がることはおろか). B is marked by も, さえ, まで, すら.\n\nIt can also be used in affirmative sentences with まで/も for escalation (*not only A but even B*): 彼は英語はおろか、アラビア語まで話せる — but the negative use is the core one.\n\nCompare:\n- **〜どころか** (N2): *far from ~ / let alone ~*; more colloquial, and can also mean *on the contrary* (休むどころか、倍働いた). はおろか never means *on the contrary*.\n- **〜はもちろん／〜はもとより** (N2): *not to mention A* — neutral or positive, often used in affirmative sentences; はおろか is typically negative and emotional.\n- **〜すら／〜さえ** (#25): marks only the B part.\n\nRegister: written/formal; not used for requests or suggestions (✗家賃はおろか食費も払ってください). JLPT tip: if the sentence has 〜さえ／すら〜ない and the first noun is a 'bigger' thing, はおろか fits.",
