          "deepDive": "**AそばからB** (そば = *right beside*) = *no sooner is A done than B (again)*. Its hallmark is **repetition**: A and B happen again and again in a cycle (作るそばから食べる, 払うそばから積もる, 完成するそばから出荷される). Very often B undoes A, so the effort is wasted and the speaker is frustrated: 片付けたそばから散らかす, 覚えるそばから忘れる.\n\nConnection: V-る or V-た (言ったそばから *right after saying it* is a common fixed phrase — the Check: 無駄遣いはやめると言ったそばから…).\n\nContrast with the one-time 'as soon as' patterns: **なり** (#28) and **が早いか** (#30) describe a single occurrence; そばから describes a habitual or repeated pattern. The やってみよう checks this: 説明するそばから新しい質問を出す (every time), 返信するそばから次のメールが入ってくる, 入荷するそばから売れてしまう (so it's always in short supply).\n\nAlso compare **〜たびに** (N3, *every time*, neutral; no immediacy) and **〜とたん** (single event). Pitfall: it's not used for future plans or requests. JLPT tip: look for signs of repetition (どんどん, 次々, 常に, いつも) or wasted effort.",
